INT. WKRT RADIO STATION - ON-AIR BOOTH - NIGHT

A small studio with foam-padded walls and a console of dials. A red ON AIR light glows over the glass. Through the window, a dim control room.

JUNO FAIRWEATHER, 30s, the late-night host, in headphones and a cardigan, leans toward her microphone. Across the console sits her producer, THEODORE "TEDDY" PAK, 30s, nervous, holding a clipboard. On a stool by the door, ROSALIE MENDOZA-CLARK, 20s, a caller who has come in person, clutches a paper cup of coffee.

JUNO
(into mic)
You're listening to the Midnight Hour. Our next caller is on line two. Hello, line two.

DAVID (V.O.)
(over phone)
Juno, hi. Long-time listener. I'm calling about someone in the room.

Juno's eyes flick to Teddy. Teddy shakes his head, mouthing "no." Rosalie goes pale.

JUNO
(into mic)
Is that right.

DAVID (V.O.)
(over phone)
I want to say something to Rosalie.

ROSALIE
(rising)
Turn it off.

JUNO
Rosalie.

TEDDY
Juno.

They speak at the same time.

JUNO
I can't cut a caller on air.

TEDDY ^
Cut the line, cut the line!

Rosalie shakes her head and takes a step toward the console.

DAVID (V.O.)
(over phone)
I was a coward. I'm sorry. Will you marry me?

Silence. The red light burns. Juno slowly slides her headphones off one ear. Teddy drops the clipboard. Rosalie stares at the speaker, then bursts into tears and laughter, and takes the microphone from Juno's hands.

ROSALIE
(into mic)
Ask me in person.
